# Fix mojibake (double-encoded UTF-8)

**Goal:** Repair source files whose UTF-8 text was decoded as cp1252 and re-saved —
Persian renders as `Ø·ÙˆÙ„`, arrows as `â†’`, em-dashes as `â€”` in the UI.

## Steps
1. Scan: `python scripts\fix_mojibake.py --dry-run` (from repo root).
2. Apply: `python scripts\fix_mojibake.py` — repeat until dry-run says 0 files
   (some corruption is multi-layer).
3. Verify: no line contains U+0080–U+00FF / U+0100-range cp1252 specials:
   `python -c` check with `sys.stdout.buffer.write(...encode('utf-8'))`.
4. Run `docker exec helix-api sh -c "cd /app/backend && python manage.py test api"`, then
   `powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\install-local-docker.ps1`.

## Gotchas
- Python console on Windows is cp1252: printing Persian *always* shows mojibake.
  Verify via `sys.stdout.buffer.write(text.encode('utf-8'))`, not `print`.
- C1 controls (U+0080–U+009F, e.g. U+008C from cp1252-undefined bytes) are part of
  mojibake runs; treating only U+00A0–U+00FF as suspect splits runs and leaves `Û±`-style leftovers.
- Do not gate repair runs on markers like `â`/`Ø`: short runs (e.g. `Û±`) contain none.
- The Docker volume `markdown-files` may self-heal at startup via
  `sync_pipeline_agents_from_source`; check the container (`grep -rl 'â€' /app/backend/markdown-files`)
  before editing volume copies by hand.
- Suspect-set repair must be strict: try `cp1252` per char, fall back to `latin-1`,
  then require a valid UTF-8 decode of the whole run or keep it unchanged.

## What didn't work
- Whole-file `text.encode('cp1252').decode('utf-8')` — fails when legit Persian/Unicode
  coexists with mojibake.
- One repair pass — triple-encoded sequences need repeated runs.
